WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.040
Series under the surface season 1 episode 1.
Have you ever wondered what secrets the Earth hides beneath its surface?

00:10.040 --> 00:14.480
In the realm of Earth science, one of the most fascinating subjects is the study of

00:14.480 --> 00:15.960
caves.

00:15.960 --> 00:20.280
These subterranean wonders are not just hollow spaces in the ground.

00:20.280 --> 00:26.680
They are geological marvels, etched out by the forces of nature over countless millennia.

00:26.680 --> 00:31.960
There are several types of caves, each with its distinct characteristics.

00:31.960 --> 00:37.520
Solution caves, formed by the dissolution of soluble rocks, are often rich in stunning

00:37.520 --> 00:38.520
formations.

00:38.520 --> 00:44.320
Lava caves, on the other hand, are born from the fiery wrath of volcanoes.

00:44.320 --> 00:49.200
Then we have sea caves, sculpted by the relentless waves of the ocean.

00:49.200 --> 00:51.500
But how are these caves formed?

00:51.500 --> 00:55.800
The answer lies in the processes of erosion and weathering.

00:55.800 --> 01:01.040
Over time, water, wind and other natural elements gradually wear away the rock, carving

01:01.040 --> 01:04.640
out these hidden chambers beneath the Earth's surface.

01:04.640 --> 01:09.780
The formation of caves is a testament to the Earth's ability to sculpt itself, creating

01:09.780 --> 01:12.000
wonders that leave us in awe.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.080
Imagine walking beneath a ceiling adorned with thousands of natural tapering structures.

01:18.080 --> 01:20.840
Welcome to the world of stalactites.

01:20.840 --> 01:24.920
These fascinating formations are nature's slow artistry.

01:24.920 --> 01:28.200
Each one a masterpiece formed drop by drop.

01:28.200 --> 01:33.380
They begin as mere mineral-rich water seeping through the ceiling of a cave.

01:33.380 --> 01:38.360
As each droplet dangles from the ceiling, it leaves behind a tiny deposit of calcium

01:38.360 --> 01:43.320
carbonate, the primary ingredient in this natural sculpture.

01:43.320 --> 01:48.120
Over time, and we're talking about a lot of time here, these deposits accumulate, growing

01:48.120 --> 01:52.380
downwards into the tapering structures we know as stalactites.

01:52.380 --> 01:57.940
This process, painstakingly slow, can take anywhere from a few thousand to tens of thousands

01:57.940 --> 01:58.980
of years.

01:58.980 --> 02:04.780
Each stalactite is a testament to the passage of time, an ageless chronicle etched in stone.

02:04.780 --> 02:10.220
From a distance, stalactites may seem uniform, but up close, their intricate formations and

02:10.220 --> 02:15.940
unique patterns captivate our imaginations, serving as a vivid reminder of nature's patience

02:15.940 --> 02:17.980
and persistency.

02:18.380 --> 02:21.780
Ever notice the stone icicles reaching upwards?

02:21.780 --> 02:23.780
Those are stalagmites.

02:23.780 --> 02:29.500
These geological wonders are born from a slow, meticulous process that takes thousands, even

02:29.500 --> 02:31.700
millions of years.

02:31.700 --> 02:37.300
Imagine a water droplet, heavy with minerals, making its journey from the cave ceiling only

02:37.300 --> 02:39.300
to land on the floor.

02:39.300 --> 02:43.300
The stalagmites are the most important elements of the stalagmites.

02:44.300 --> 02:50.300
With each drop, it leaves behind a minute deposit of its mineral cargo, mainly calcium

02:50.300 --> 02:52.300
carbonate.

02:52.300 --> 02:58.300
Over centuries, these tiny deposits accumulate, growing millimetre by millimetre, creating

02:58.300 --> 03:00.300
a stalagmite.

03:00.300 --> 03:05.300
Despite their snail's pace of growth, some stalagmites have reached astonishing heights,

03:05.300 --> 03:09.300
with the tallest recorded one towering at over 28 metres.

03:09.300 --> 03:12.300
It's a breathtaking spectacle, isn't it?

03:12.300 --> 03:16.300
A testament to the patience and persistence of nature's craft.

03:16.300 --> 03:19.300
So the next time you find yourself in a cave, look down.

03:19.300 --> 03:24.300
You'll be standing next to a monument that took nature eons to erect.

03:24.300 --> 03:29.300
Stalagmites, rising from the ground, stand as a testament to the power of time and the

03:29.300 --> 03:31.300
persistence of nature.

03:31.300 --> 03:37.300
From the captivating stalactites to the towering stalagmites, caves are more than just a

03:38.300 --> 03:42.300
hollow spaces in the ground.

03:42.300 --> 03:47.300
They are dynamic galleries of the Earth's natural artistry, showcasing how water and

03:47.300 --> 03:52.300
minerals can sculpt masterpieces over thousands of years.

03:52.300 --> 03:57.300
Stalactites, those icicle-like formations hanging from cave ceilings, grow painstakingly

03:57.300 --> 04:03.300
slow, with each droplet of mineral-rich water adding a tiny layer to their length.

04:03.300 --> 04:09.300
Their counterparts, the stalagmites, rise from the cave floor, built by the drips falling

04:09.300 --> 04:11.300
from their stalactite partners above.

04:11.300 --> 04:16.300
These subterranean landscapes tell a story of our planet's past, of climate changes

04:16.300 --> 04:18.300
and geological events.

04:18.300 --> 04:23.300
But they also remind us of the importance of preservation.

04:23.300 --> 04:29.300
These intricate formations, carved by nature's hand, are vulnerable to human activities and

04:29.300 --> 04:30.300
climate change.

04:30.300 --> 04:36.300
As we delve deeper into these underground wonders, we uncover more about our planet's

04:36.300 --> 04:41.300
past and its incredible ability to shape and transform over time.

04:41.300 --> 04:46.300
The secrets of the Earth, it seems, are often hidden just beneath the surface.

